    "title": "All Rolling Stones Data Posts, January 19 2026",
    "text": "All Rolling Stones Data Posts, January 19 2026\nClick on title links to read all posts published today.\nIf you like what I do, help keep it going! ROLLING STONES DATA is a fan’s page for fans, ad-free, not affiliated to the band, and it runs on the support of readers like you. Your donation helps cover costs and keeps fresh Stones content coming your way. Thank you!\nRolling Stones chronology: January 19\nJanuary 19 holds significant moments in Rolling Stones history, featuring Ronnie Wood’s involvement with the Jeff Beck Group and Keith Richards’ collaborations, shaping the band’s evolving legacy over the years.Cut-Up Lyrics: The Rolling Stones’ ‘Casino Boogie’ (1972)\n‘Casino Boogie’, a track from the Rolling Stones’ Exile on Main St., embodies a chaotic, instinctual creation process influenced by William Burroughs’ cut-up technique, reflecting the band’s turbulent lifestyle and emotions during their exile.‘Send It to Me’: Rolling Stones con Algo de Reggae (1980)\n‘Send It to Me’ de los Rolling Stones es una canción caracterizada por el humor y la soledad de Jagger, fusionando ritmos jamaiquinos y letras que abordan la búsqueda de conexión emocional.The Rolling Stones: ‘Emotional Rescue’ Long Version (1979)\nThis unreleased long version of ‘Emotional Rescue’ showcases The Rolling Stones’ playful experimentation in the studio, highlighting Mick Jagger’s creative control and the band’s spontaneous musical exploration during 1979 sessions.A Rolling Stones’ Detour: Mick Jagger Films ‘Ned Kelly’ (1969)\nMick Jagger’s role as Ned Kelly in the 1970 film transformed his creative process, leading to the development of iconic song “Brown Sugar” during a pivotal period away from the Rolling Stones.Charlie Watts: Why The Rolling Stones Still Work (2013)\nCharlie Watts attributed the Rolling Stones’ lasting appeal to Mick Jagger’s stage presence and Keith Richards’ musical skills, emphasizing their unique chemistry and dedication as key to the band’s enduring magnetism.\nCATEGORIES (updated daily):Today in Stones’ History (Daily chronology 1962-present)Can You Hear the Music? (All official songs 1963-present with song details, recording info, historical facts, and more)Flashback (The Stones live through the years)Unreleased (Lost and found! Unreleased songs 1962-present with recording info, song details, and more)Watch This All Together (Lost and found footage through the years)Articles (assorted pieces on the band)Yesterday’s Papers (The Stones in the press 1962-present)Stones Quotes (quotes from the boys through the years)\nAlso: Subscribe to Stones Data on Facebook here",
